//Brute Force 

Approach: 
Initialize a fixed-size result array of size 2.
Iterate through the array using index i.
For each element, iterate through the array using index j to check all pairs.
If nums[i] + nums[j] == target (ensuring i != j), record the indices and return the result.

Time Complexity - O(n^2) - Nested loops check every combination of elements.
Space Complexity - O(1) - Uses a fixed-size array requiring no extra scaling memory.

class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        int[] arr=new int[2];
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
            int ans=target-nums[i];
            for(int j=0;j<nums.length;j++){
                if(nums[j]==ans && i!=j){
                    arr[0]=i;
                    arr[1]=j;
                }
            }
        }
        return arr;
    }
}



//Optimal Solution

Solve in a single pass by dynamically tracking previously visited elements and their indices in a Hash Map to check for the required complement instantly.

Approach: 
Initialize an empty Hash Map to store element -> index mappings.
Iterate through the array once using index i.
Calculate the required complement: res = target - nums[i].
Check if the Hash Map contains res:
If found: Retrieve its index, assign the indices to the result array, and return.
Else: Store the current element and its index in the map for future lookups.

Time Complexity - O(n)
Space Complexity - O(n)

class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        int[] arr = new int[2];
        HashMap<Integer,Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
            int req = target - nums[i];
            if(map.containsKey(req)){
                int first = map.get(req);
                arr[0] = first;
                arr[1] = i;
            }else{
                map.put(nums[i], i);
            }
        }
        return arr;
    }
}